<!-- Phase 5 website teaching layer; original chapter remains read-only. Evidence: website/reviews/android-phase5-findings.md. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A profile request succeeds in a demo. In production, six requests hit an expired token together, refresh traffic competes with ordinary traffic, and a debug logger consumes a response before the converter sees it. The useful question is who owns each boundary: the API contract, the HTTP exchange, the credential, and the response body.</p>

<!-- @after Mental Model :: visual -->
### Follow the ownership, not just the URL

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong>API contract</strong><small>Retrofit maps the declared endpoint and parameters into a call.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Representation</strong><small>A configured converter handles request or response bodies.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>HTTP exchange</strong><small>OkHttp owns transport, connection reuse and HTTP middleware.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Application policy</strong><small>Your code owns replay safety, credential lifecycle and domain errors.</small></div></div><figcaption>A successful HTTP exchange is only one part of a successful product operation.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @before Architecture :: review -->
The converter and GZIP labels in the next diagram (RT1) are under technical review. A correction is pending the owner's approval.

<!-- @after Production Example :: animation -->
### Many 401s, one refresh decision

<figure class="stepper" data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Trace two requests using an expired credential</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="a"><strong>Request A</strong><small data-text="aV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="b"><strong>Request B</strong><small data-text="bV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="token"><strong>Token storage</strong><small data-text="tokenV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="refresh"><strong>Dedicated refresh API</strong><small data-text="refreshV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{
"label": "Expired token",
"caption": "Both calls leave with token T1. This is a schematic successful refresh path, not a promise that every 401 is recoverable.",
"state": {
"a": "active",
"b": "active"
},
"text": {
"aV": "sent T1",
"bV": "sent T1",
"tokenV": "T1",
"refreshV": "idle"
},
"add": [
"a",
"b",
"token",
"refresh"
]
},
{
"label": "A wins the refresh",
"caption": "The authenticator serializes access, checks stored credentials and makes one bounded refresh call.",
"state": {
"a": "warn",
"refresh": "active"
},
"text": {
"aV": "401 → refresh decision",
"refreshV": "request new credential"
}
},
{
"label": "A stores T2",
"caption": "After a successful refresh, A stores T2 and returns a follow-up request.",
"state": {
"token": "done",
"a": "active",
"refresh": "done"
},
"text": {
"tokenV": "T2",
"aV": "follow-up uses T2",
"refreshV": "finished"
}
},
{
"label": "B rechecks",
"caption": "B reaches the same decision later. Storage differs from its failed Authorization header, so it can reuse T2.",
"state": {
"b": "active"
},
"text": {
"bV": "follow-up uses T2"
}
},
{
"label": "Bound the outcome",
"caption": "If recovery fails or a further challenge exceeds the allowed count, return no follow-up. The repository must handle the failure.",
"state": {
"a": "done",
"b": "done"
},
"text": {
"aV": "success or terminal failure",
"bV": "success or terminal failure"
}
}
]</script><figcaption>This traces the authenticator example above. Use a separate refresh client and test concurrent challenges, cancellation and refresh failure.</figcaption></figure>

